    <!-- https://w3id.org/pmd/cryo/CRYO_0000164 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/pmd/cryo/CRYO_0000164">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#topObjectProperty"/>
        <rdfs:range>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/pmd/cryo/CRYO_0000021"/>
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/pmd/cryo/CRYO_0000023"/>
                </owl:unionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </rdfs:range>
        <rdfs:comment>Intentially not a sub-property of &quot;denoted by&quot; as this rescticts using &quot;denoted by&quot; in property chains of its sub-properties</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>The sub-properties are inferrable through corresponding property chains</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">denoted by family</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">denoted by family is a top-level object property between a crystallographic entity, i.e., plane or direction, and the family of Miller indices</skos:definition>
        <skos:example xml:lang="en">Consider a (011) crystallographic plane in cubic lattice, i.e., the instance of the plane is denoted by (011) instance. The instance of the plane is be inferred to be of type &quot;crystallographic entity in cubic system&quot; and carries &quot;is cubic&quot; reftexive triple. The (011) is a member of {110} family of Miller indices. The property chain &quot;is cubic + denoted by + member of&quot; will infer that the plane is denoted by {110} in a cubic lattice.</skos:example>
    </owl:ObjectProperty>
    


